      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: So you are saying that in the -- within 2244(d)(1), those words are surplusage, they don't do anything, that -- the -- that 2244 would mean the same thing if there were a period after \"became final.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Roberts",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1500",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d21b53158b853c4e053f6b3d273f1dae62cc5d87e7a1d0812ffaf05e68e4affb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d21b53158b85-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One, there's a background rule, and the presumption is generally of -- of what -- when a judgment becomes final in the collateral review context, and it's generally presumed, with good reason, that Congress legislates against that background rule and uses the terms with their settled meaning in that context; and second, we know Congress did that with respect to State prisoners in section 2244 because they clarified it there; and it makes sense that the time limitation should run at the same time, because there's no persuasive reason for them to run"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1500",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d21b53158b853c4e053f6b3d273f1dae62cc5d87e7a1d0812ffaf05e68e4affb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d21b53158b85-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, usually variations in language mean variations in meaning."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1500",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d21b53158b853c4e053f6b3d273f1dae62cc5d87e7a1d0812ffaf05e68e4affb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d21b53158b85-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If there are no further questions, we would submit."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1500",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "d21b53158b853c4e053f6b3d273f1dae62cc5d87e7a1d0812ffaf05e68e4affb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d21b53158b85-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But you admit that for one part of that clause, Congress didn't mean any different. The -- if there is a petition filed, if there is, in fact, a cert petition filed, then State and Federal prisoners got treated alike, so it's the -- the only place, as I understand it, where you're saying there's a difference is whether the time for filing a petition counts even when the -- there -- no petition is filed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. DE BRUIN",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1500",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "d21b53158b853c4e053f6b3d273f1dae62cc5d87e7a1d0812ffaf05e68e4affb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d21b53158b85-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DE BRUIN: It could mean that, and that was, in fact, the established rule under Rule 33, which is a"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1500",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "d21b53158b853c4e053f6b3d273f1dae62cc5d87e7a1d0812ffaf05e68e4affb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d21b53158b85-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Am I wrong in thinking that the general understanding is that when you file a cert petition, that the finality is suspended until that petition is disposed of?"
    },
